<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
An operator reconciles custom resources that its users write. A string field of a custom
resource is therefore untrusted input: anyone who can create or edit that resource — often
a much larger group than the cluster administrators, since namespace-scoped
<code>edit</code> on a CRD is a common grant — controls its exact contents.
</p>

<p>
This query reports a string field of a type registered as a custom resource with
<code>sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/scheme</code> whose value reaches, through taint,
a SQL or NoSQL query sink. If that value is concatenated into query text rather than passed
as a bound parameter, the author of the custom resource can inject arbitrary query syntax
and read, modify, or destroy data in the operator's database.
</p>

<p>
The result is a path from the field read to the query sink, so it can be checked whether
the value is really interpolated into the query or merely passed alongside it.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Use parameterized queries. Pass the value as a query argument (<code>?</code> or
<code>$1</code> with <code>database/sql</code>) rather than building the statement with
<code>fmt.Sprintf</code> or string concatenation, so the database driver never parses it as
query syntax. For a NoSQL driver, build the filter as a typed document rather than from a
string.
</p>

<p>
Where a value cannot be a bound parameter — a table or column name, a sort direction —
validate it against a fixed allowlist of permitted values before it reaches the query.
</p>

<p>
Independently of the query itself, constrain what a custom resource may contain: an
OpenAPI <code>pattern</code> or <code>enum</code> in the CRD schema, or a validating
admission webhook, rejects malformed values before the operator ever sees them. Treat this
as defense in depth, not as a replacement for parameterization.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the operator builds a query out of a field taken straight from
the custom resource it is reconciling.
</p>

<sample language="go">
func (r *WidgetReconciler) rows(ctx context.Context, widget *v1alpha1.Widget) (*sql.Rows, error) {
	// BAD: a user-controlled field of the custom resource is concatenated into the query.
	q := "SELECT id, value FROM metrics WHERE tenant = '" + widget.Spec.Tenant + "'"
	return r.DB.QueryContext(ctx, q)
}
</sample>

<p>
In the corrected version the field is passed as a bound parameter, so the driver treats it
as a value and never as query syntax.
</p>

<sample language="go">
func (r *WidgetReconciler) rows(ctx context.Context, widget *v1alpha1.Widget) (*sql.Rows, error) {
	// GOOD: the value is bound as a parameter.
	const q = "SELECT id, value FROM metrics WHERE tenant = $1"
	return r.DB.QueryContext(ctx, q, widget.Spec.Tenant)
}
</sample>
</example>

